The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (__mptcp_check_fallback(msk)) {
    spin_unlock_bh(&msk->fallback_lock);
    ...
}
Reason:
The condition only checked `__mptcp_check_fallback(msk)`, which may be false when an infinite fallback is in progress but subflows are already disallowed (`msk->allow_subflows == false`). In that scenario, `__mptcp_retrans()` proceeds to call `mptcp_sendmsg_frag()`, which can invoke `__mptcp_try_fallback()` and attempt to reacquire `msk->fallback_lock`, leading to a recursive locking deadlock because the lock is already held by the caller.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (__mptcp_check_fallback(msk) ||
    !msk->allow_subflows) {
    spin_unlock_bh(&msk->fallback_lock);
    ...
}
Reason:
The additional condition `!msk->allow_subflows` ensures that when an infinite fallback is in progress (i.e., no new subflows are allowed), the retransmission is skipped before attempting any packet send that could trigger a fallback and cause a double-lock on `fallback_lock`. This atomic check prevents the deadlock by avoiding the reinjection in the critical fallback scenario.

